These are the commands that will work for uploading your **current `myapp` folder** to GitHub and replacing the remote repo:

```powershell
cd C:\Users\taiba\my-app\vendor\laravel\simpleauth\myapp
```

Remove old Git connection (if wrong):

```powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force .git
```

Initialize fresh Git:

```powershell
git init
```

Add GitHub repository:

```powershell
git remote add origin https://github.com/webmaster1897/3auth.git
```

Add all files:

```powershell
git add .
```

Commit:

```powershell
git commit -m "Upload complete Laravel project"
```

Set branch:

```powershell
git branch -M main
```

Upload and overwrite GitHub:

```powershell
git push -u origin main --force
```

After this, refresh your GitHub repo. Your project files will be uploaded.

For Laravel, do this from the folder that contains:

```
app
bootstrap
config
database
public
resources
routes
artisan
composer.json
package.json
```





From your history, these are the **commands that actually worked** for uploading the complete Laravel project to a new GitHub repository.

### 1. Go inside your Laravel project

```powershell
cd C:\Users\taiba\my-app\vendor\laravel\simpleauth\myapp
```

### 2. Check existing remote

```powershell
git remote -v
```

### 3. Change old GitHub repository to new one

(Instead of adding a new remote)

```powershell
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/webmaster1897/3auth.git
```

Verify:

```powershell
git remote -v
```

### 4. Check branch

```powershell
git branch
```

If needed:

```powershell
git branch -M main
```

### 5. Add all project files

```powershell
git add .
```

### 6. Commit changes

```powershell
git commit -m "Upload complete Laravel project"
```

### 7. Push to new repository

Because GitHub already had another commit, normal push failed:

```
! [rejected] main -> main (fetch first)
```

So you used force push:

```powershell
git push -u origin main --force
```

✅ This replaced the GitHub repository content with your local Laravel project.

